<h2>Same Perimeter, Different Shapes</h2>
<p>Original source A–J figures and the open dot-grid tasks are linked, not copied: <a href="https://cms-assets.illustrativemathematics.org/gxg4e306ybbahjpn98ovmoyk46xz" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">2021 original four-page student PDF</a>.</p>
<p><strong>All 10 source figure perimeters</strong> were checked by tracing PDF vector boundary segments on a common dot grid. You may choose any three to explain, as the teacher's original task requires; not all need be attempted.</p>
<table class="source-chart"><thead><tr><th>Original figures</th><th>Perimeter (dot-grid units)</th><th>Important concept</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>A, G, H</td><td>18</td><td>Different silhouettes, equal boundary length</td></tr>
<tr><td>B, E</td><td>16</td><td>Square and thin rectangle can share perimeter</td></tr>
<tr><td>C, D, I</td><td>10</td><td>Notches must be counted too</td></tr>
<tr><td>F, J</td><td>22</td><td>Two different concave forms</td></tr></tbody></table>
<p>First, check the four original true/false multi-digit identities: false, true, false, true. For the open tasks, draw two <strong>different</strong> shapes with each target boundary 12, 26 or 48 units. Use only horizontal and vertical segments between dots, not diagonals, and make each outline closed.</p>
<p>Typed numerical checks are mathematical anchors, not a substitute for teacher checks of the sketch, target, uniqueness and partner explanation. The publisher's original Cool-down is gated; Edulytics exit practice is independently authored.</p>
